Two termios fixes in telnet/sys_bsd.c for cosmo.

1. sys_bsd.c picks "encoded (4.2BSD) vs numeric (4.4BSD)" baud rates via
   `#if B4800 != 4800` -> DECODE_BAUD, which builds a static termspeeds[]
   table out of B0/B50/.../B230400. On cosmo those B* are
   `extern const uint32_t` (runtime values, not macros), so the preprocessor
   reads B4800 as 0 (DECODE_BAUD on) and the table fails to compile
   (non-constant static initializers). Terminal line speed is cosmetic for a
   networked client on Windows, so DECODE_BAUD stays off under
   __COSMOPOLITAN__ and TerminalSpeeds passes cfgetospeed()/cfgetispeed()
   through.

2. TerminalFlushOutput falls back to the Linux `TCFLSH` ioctl when
   <sys/ioctl.h> has no TIOCFLUSH. cosmo has neither ioctl number but does
   provide the POSIX tcflush()/TCIOFLUSH; use that.

--- a/telnet/sys_bsd.c
+++ b/telnet/sys_bsd.c
@@ -253,7 +253,7 @@
 #ifdef	TIOCFLUSH
   ioctl (fileno (stdout), TIOCFLUSH, &flags);
 #else
-  ioctl (fileno (stdout), TCFLSH, &flags);
+  tcflush (fileno (stdout), TCIOFLUSH); (void) flags;
 #endif
 }
 
@@ -777,7 +777,7 @@
 /*
  * Try to guess whether speeds are "encoded" (4.2BSD) or just numeric (4.4BSD).
  */
-#if B4800 != 4800
+#if B4800 != 4800 && !defined __COSMOPOLITAN__
 # define DECODE_BAUD
 #endif
 
